{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.OffDiagNonvanishing","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.OffDiagNonvanishing","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.AxialDistanceBound"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag_axialDist_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag_axialDist_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/OffDiagNonvanishing.lean#L114-L131","doc":"**Admissible swaps carry a nonzero off-diagonal entry.**  Combines\n`TableauCells.two_le_natAbs_axialDist` with `offDiag_eq_zero_iff_eq_neg_one`: an admissible swap\nis at distance `|d| ≥ 2`, and `offDiag` vanishes only at `d = -1`.\n\nRetargeted from `eq_one_or_neg_one_of_offDiag_eq_zero` (bean `qou-y4ca`). The disjunction\nobliged this proof to dispatch a `d = 1` case that cannot occur — `offDiag q 1 = 1 ≠ 0` — so\nthe `rcases … <;> omega` discharged one real case and one vacuous one. The biconditional\nsupplies `d = -1` directly. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag_eq_zero_iff_eq_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.offDiag_eq_zero_iff_eq_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/OffDiagNonvanishing.lean#L88-L112","doc":"**`offDiag` vanishes exactly at `d = -1`** — the biconditional, and it is *stronger* than\nthe disjunction `eq_one_or_neg_one_of_offDiag_eq_zero` concludes.\n\nThe `d = 1` disjunct is never realised: `offDiag` takes the plain branch for every `d ≥ 0`\n(`offDiag_of_not_neg`), so `offDiag q 1 = 1 ≠ 0` in any field. Only the negative branch can\nvanish, and there the two-to-one argument leaves `d = -1` alone.\n\nThis is the honest `_iff_` for this entry, and it is **not** the one the declaration's old name\n`offDiag_eq_zero_iff` promised. That name suggested `offDiag q d = 0 ↔ d = 1 ∨ d = -1`, whose\nreverse direction is **false** at `d = 1` — so no amount of work could have produced it. Bean\n`qou-y4ca` asked whether that converse was true; it is not, and this is what the corpus licenses\ninstead. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.eq_one_or_neg_one_of_offDiag_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.SeminormalRep.eq_one_or_neg_one_of_offDiag_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":43,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/OffDiagNonvanishing.lean#L43-L86","doc":"**`offDiag` vanishes only at `d = ±1`.**  Equivalently: away from the scalar (same-row /\nsame-column) distances the off-diagonal entry of the generator matrix is nonzero. ","signatureChunk":0}]}